    "document_id": "d3917ba1-0293-43d2-93ae-5cd52fd17db3",
    "origin_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/10/15/tesla-unveils-cyberfied-new-roadster-kickflip/",
    "collected_at": "2026-10-16T06:51:33.743363+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://electrek.co/2026/10/15/tesla-unveils-cyberfied-new-roadster-kickflip/",
    "canonical_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/10/15/tesla-unveils-cyberfied-new-roadster-kickflip/",
    "origin_publisher": "Electrek",
    "published_at": "2026-10-16T01:43:32+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-16T01:43:32+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-16T06:52:37.234118+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-16T06:52:37.234118+00:00",
    "body": "Tesla has unveiled a new version of its next-generation Roadster. As expected, it received Tesla’s latest design “cyberfication” and cold-gas thrusters to allow it to jump and flip through the air.\nThe livestream featured impressive stunts but offered few details. Tesla released more information on X after the event, but there’s still no price and no production date.\nNine years in the making\nTesla first unveiled the next-gen Roadster as a prototype in November 2017 and promised production in 2020. That date came and went, and so did several more.\nThis year alone, Elon Musk promised an unveil in April , then late April, then “a month or so” after Q1 earnings. The demo was later pushed to August , then to October 1. Tesla finally streamed the event tonight.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nThe entire intro felt like Elon Musk and co. trying to create a reason to link Tesla and SpaceX ahead of an expected merger. It gave Solar Tile unveiling event ahead of Tesla’s SolarCity acquisition.\nFrom S3XY to Cyber\nTesla confirmed that the design changed a lot since the 2017 prototype. Its design language evolved from the S3XY lineup to Cybertruck, Cybercab, and Optimus, and that the new Roadster takes inspiration from the SR-71 and the F-35.\nImage: Tesla\nOn its updated Roadster page , Tesla describes hidden headlights, a full-length taillight, and flush-mounted aero. The “Ano White” paint is said to borrow from coatings used to protect rockets, and the tinted windows are meant to mimic the gold visor of a spacesuit.\nImage: Tesla\nInside, there’s a yoke designed for steer-by-wire, a center touchscreen, and two smaller displays in front of the driver. Tesla also went with a fixed seat position and manual controls to save weight, along with magnesium, titanium, and carbon fiber throughout.\nThree versions: Performance, Thrust, and Rocket\nThe configurations only came out after the stream, in a thread on Tesla’s X account . There are three:\nPerformance: road ready, seats two, active aero and a “multi-mode suction system.” 0-60 mph in under 1.6 seconds. Available by reservation.\nThrust: race ready, seats two, adds rear-mounted cold-gas thrusters. 0-60 mph in under 0.9 seconds. Limited release.\nRocket: seats one, adds thrusters around the perimeter of the car for upward and rotational movement. 0-60 mph in under 0.59 seconds and a top speed of over 230 mph. Invite only.\nTesla quotes a range of over 300 miles. The company’s own Roadster page states that the Rocket isn’t approved for public road use, and Tesla treats Thrust and Rocket as “augments” on top of the Performance car.\nImage: Tesla\nThe thrusters are where SpaceX comes in. Tesla says making them work in atmosphere rather than a vacuum required new hardware and controls, and that every usable void in the car is packed with carbon-fiber overwrapped pressure vessels (COPVs) to store compressed gas. More gas means more “flight time.”\nTesla claims the thrusters generate four to five times the force the tires can handle, which is how it gets around the traction limit that caps every other car’s acceleration.\nThe suction system does the opposite job. Tesla says its fans pull enough air from under the car to hold it upside down, even when stationary. In Track Mode , the car lowers itself and deploys active wings driven by the same actuators Tesla developed for Optimus. Tesla claims downforce “approaching F1 levels.”\nImage: Tesla\nWhat’s still missing\nTesla reopened Roadster reservations. It’s $5,000 due today, which Tesla calls fully refundable, and another $45,000 due within 10 days or the reservation gets canceled. That’s the same $50,000 Tesla asked for in 2017.\nBut Tesla didn’t give a price for any of the three versions, and there’s no production date other than “2028, actually”, an actual quote from Musk.\nSome of the specs have also moved since 2017. Tesla originally promised 620 miles of range, a top speed above 250 mph, and seating for four in a 2+2 layout. The new car is now quoted at over 300 miles, over 230 mph on the Rocket trim, and two seats at most.\nAcceleration went the other way. The base Performance trim beats the 1.9-second 0-60 mph Tesla promised in 2017, and the Thrust and Rocket claims are well beyond anything on the market, including the Rimac Nevera.\nAnd it can also do impressive stunts:\nRoadster event livestream https://t.co/G0jU4mWfid — Tesla (@Tesla) October 15, 2026\nI doubt using those features will keep your warranty going, though.\nElectrek’s Take\nThe engineering here is real. Cold-gas thrusters that work in atmosphere, a fan system that can hold a car to a ceiling, and wings run by Optimus actuators. Nothing entirely new, but I think it’s the first time that it’s all in one car, which is impressive.\nBut the stunts that are making the headlines are on trims that are invite-only or limited, and not road legal. They will likley null your warranty, and at the end of the day, I doubt Tesla will sell more than a handful of those for marketing reasons only.\nTop comment by Philip234\nLiked by 33 people\nSo in a nutshell: less range and less performance than the Denza Z or the Xiaomi SU7 Ultra (both on sale in Europe next year) but at twice the price. Well done Elon, well done.\nView all comments\nAnd it’s hard to ignore that the range claim got cut roughly in half from what Tesla promised nine years ago, along with two seats. Tesla collected $50,000 to $250,000 deposits from customers back then on the strength of those specs.\nNow, what do those who wanted the originally unveiled new Roadster do?\nDisclosure: I hold two Founders Series Roadster referral awards from Tesla’s old referral program and have been waiting since.\nCharge your electric vehicle at home using rooftop solar panels. Find a reliable and competitively-priced solar installer near you on EnergySage for free. They have hundreds of pre-vetted installers competing for your business, ensuring you get high-quality solutions and save 20-30% compared to going it alone. Plus, it’s free to use, and you won’t get sales calls until you select an installer and share your phone number with them. Your personalized solar quotes are easy to compare online, and you’ll get access to unbiased Energy Advisers to help you every step of the way. Get started here .\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on Twitter , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
